{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Beta diversity (community dissimilarity) among reefs has decreased over the survey period (2005–2020), indicating biogeographic homogenization of reef fish assemblages in the Gulf of California.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether average pairwise community dissimilarity (Bray–Curtis) among reefs declines over time, signaling homogenization.",  
      "steps":"1. For each survey year, pivot the data to a reef × species matrix of biomass (fill absent species with zero).  2. For each year, compute the Bray–Curtis dissimilarity between all pairs of reefs (e.g. using scipy or skbio).  3. Calculate the mean dissimilarity per year.  4. Fit a linear regression of mean dissimilarity vs year to test for a negative slope.  5. Validate results with a non-parametric Mann–Kendall trend test on the annual mean dissimilarity.  6. Optionally, repeat separately for protected vs unprotected subsets to compare homogenization rates.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Time series plot of mean Bray–Curtis dissimilarity vs year with trend line and confidence interval. 2. Regression summary with slope estimate, p-value, and R². 3. Mann–Kendall test statistic and p-value. 4. Table of results for protected vs unprotected reefs."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Shallow strata (e.g. 0–10 m) show larger declines in species richness and biomass during warm anomaly years than deeper strata (e.g. 10–20 m), reflecting greater thermal stress in shallow zones.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether depth stratum modulates the impact of warming anomalies on fish diversity and biomass.",  
      "steps":"1. Classify each transect record into 'shallow' or 'deep' based on its depth_stratum column. 2. Define anomaly years (2014–2016) and baseline years (2010–2013). 3. Aggregate species richness and total biomass per reef × period × stratum. 4. Fit two-way ANOVA or mixed-effects model: response (richness or biomass) ~ period * stratum + (1|reef). 5. Test for significant period×stratum interaction. 6. Perform post-hoc contrasts comparing anomaly vs baseline within each stratum.",  
      "deliverables":"1. ANOVA or mixed-model table showing main effects and interaction. 2. Interaction plot of mean richness and biomass by period for shallow vs deep. 3. Post-hoc contrast table with adjusted p-values."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Spatial variability of biomass across transects within the same reef (measured as coefficient of variation) has increased over time, indicating patchier fish distribution under environmental stress.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for temporal trend in within-reef spatial variability of biomass.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef-year, compute the mean and standard deviation of biomass across its transects. 2. Calculate the coefficient of variation (CV = SD/mean) for each reef-year. 3. Fit a linear mixed-effects model: CV ~ year + (1|reef). 4. Assess significance of the fixed effect of year. 5. Check model residuals and perform diagnostic plots. 6. Optionally, repeat separate models for protected vs unprotected reefs to compare trends.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Time series plot of CV vs year with mixed model fit. 2. Model summary with year coefficient and p-value. 3. Diagnostic plots of residuals. 4. Comparison of trends by protection status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Temporal turnover of species composition (Jaccard dissimilarity between consecutive years) at each reef has increased over the survey period, signaling greater community instability under warming.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether year-to-year species turnover per reef exhibits an increasing trend over time.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef, list species present in each survey year. 2. For each reef and each pair of consecutive years (t, t+1), compute Jaccard dissimilarity based on presence–absence. 3. Compile a dataset of turnover values tagged by mid-year (e.g. 2012.5 for 2012→2013). 4. Fit a linear mixed-effects model: turnover ~ year + (1|reef). 5. Test whether the fixed effect of year is positive. 6. Plot turnover vs time with reef-level spaghetti lines and overall trend.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mixed-effects model summary for turnover vs year. 2. Plot of turnover trends with overall regression line. 3. Distribution histogram of turnover values in early vs late periods."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The strength of the relationship between species richness and total biomass weakens after 2014, indicating decoupling of biomass production and biodiversity under warming conditions.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine whether biomass–richness slopes differ before vs after the warming anomaly.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef-year, calculate species richness and total biomass. 2. Define two periods: pre-anomaly (<=2013) and post-anomaly (>=2017). 3. Fit a linear regression with interaction: richness ~ biomass * period. 4. Compare the slope of richness vs biomass between periods (test interaction). 5. Check model assumptions and fit. 6. Visualize scatterplots of biomass vs richness colored by period with fitted lines.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Regression table showing biomass main effect, period main effect, and interaction term with p-value. 2. Scatterplot with separate regression lines for each period. 3. Summary of change in slope magnitude."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Mean biomass per species (total biomass divided by species richness) increased at unprotected reefs during the 2014–2016 anomaly, suggesting dominance by few high-biomass species despite overall richness loss.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether biomass-per-species ratio rose in unprotected reefs during the warm anomaly compared to protected reefs.",  
      "steps":"1. Aggregate total biomass and species richness per reef-period (pre, event, post). 2. Compute biomass_per_species = biomass / richness for each reef-period. 3. Fit a mixed-effects model: biomass_per_species ~ period * protection_status + (1|reef). 4. Examine period×protection interaction to see if unprotected reefs show a greater increase in ratio during event. 5. Conduct post-hoc pairwise comparisons of ratio across periods within each protection status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mixed-effects model summary for biomass_per_species. 2. Interaction plot of ratio by period and protection. 3. Post-hoc contrast table."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Skewness of biomass distribution across transects within reefs has increased over time, reflecting growing dominance of few high-biomass patches.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether the shape of transect-level biomass distributions (skewness) per reef-year has become more right-skewed over the survey period.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef-year, collect transect biomass values. 2. Compute the sample skewness statistic for each reef-year (using scipy.stats.skew). 3. Fit a linear mixed-effects model: skewness ~ year + (1|reef). 4. Test if the year coefficient is positive. 5. Plot skewness vs year with model fit.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Model summary with skewness trend. 2. Plot of skewness over time. 3. Table of average skewness in early vs late time windows."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Inter-reef variance in species richness within each region has increased over time, indicating that some reefs are more impacted by environmental change than others.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine whether spatial variance of richness among reefs within regions has grown, revealing uneven local responses.",  
      "steps":"1. Group data by region and year; compute species richness per reef-year. 2. For each region-year, calculate the variance (or standard deviation) of richness across reefs. 3. Fit separate linear regressions of regional richness-variance vs year for each region. 4. Test if slopes are positive. 5. Optionally, compare slopes across regions to identify hotspots of change.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Time series plots of richness variance per region with fitted trend lines. 2. Table of slope estimates and p-values for each region. 3. Map or table highlighting regions with strongest increase in variance."  
    }  
  }  
]}
